WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.639
Input comes from more places than most teams first expect.

00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:18.556
Web forms are obvious, but applications also receive data through API request bodies, query strings, route parameters, headers, cookies, uploaded files, webhooks, message queues, command-line arguments, environment variables, database records, and third-party integrations.

00:00:18.556 --> 00:00:22.792
Any place where data crosses a boundary deserves attention.

00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:27.028
Client-controlled fields are not limited to visible form inputs.

00:00:27.028 --> 00:00:33.684
A user can modify hidden fields, cookies, headers, JSON bodies, multipart requests, and request parameters.

00:00:33.684 --> 00:00:43.164
Mobile apps, desktop clients, browser extensions, automated scripts, and test tools can all send requests that do not follow the intended interface.

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:49.820
Server-side code must validate what it receives, not what the user interface hoped to send.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:52.845
Internal data can still be unsafe.

00:00:52.845 --> 00:01:03.536
A database record may originally have come from a user import, a partner feed, a previous software version, a support tool, or a compromised account.

00:01:03.536 --> 00:01:07.368
Message queues can carry malformed or replayed messages.

00:01:07.368 --> 00:01:12.007
Environment variables and configuration can be changed by deployment systems.

00:01:12.007 --> 00:01:18.663
Even data created inside the organization may be stale, inconsistent, or outside current business rules.

00:01:18.663 --> 00:01:24.109
A useful validation design starts by mapping input sources and trust boundaries.

00:01:24.109 --> 00:01:36.413
Ask where the data came from, who or what controls it, how it is encoded, what transformations it has already passed through, and where it will be used next.

00:01:36.413 --> 00:01:45.489
The more clearly a team understands the path of data, the easier it becomes to validate it at the right points.
